# --- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Matches docker-compose.yml. pg-boss shares this database (schema: pgboss).
#
# In production this is the INTERNAL connection URL of the Coolify PGVector 17
# database resource — Postgres is deliberately not part of docker-compose.prod.yml,
# so that Coolify's scheduled backups cover it. Reaching it by internal hostname
# needs "Connect To Predefined Network" enabled on the compose resource.
#
# Extensions and the pgboss schema are applied by `pnpm db:migrate`, so there is
# no manual setup step against a fresh database.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://voicemural:voicemural@localhost:5432/voicemural

# --- LiteLLM ----------------------------------------------------------------
# All model calls route through the LiteLLM proxy. Never hard-code model names
# in application code — reference the role-based vars below so models can be
# swapped without a code change.
LITELLM_BASE_URL=https://litellm.your-instance.example/v1
LITELLM_API_KEY=sk-...

# Role -> model mapping. Ask your LiteLLM admin for a virtual key per person so
# usage and budget are attributable.
#
# MODEL NAMES ARE INSTANCE-SPECIFIC. The CAVI proxy namespaces everything, so
# bare `whisper-1` does not exist there — it is `openai/whisper-1`. The worker
# checks these against /v1/models on startup and names the ones it can see, so
# a wrong value is reported once at boot instead of failing per chunk.
#
# Defaults below are self-hosted and open source, which keeps recordings of
# people thinking aloud on university infrastructure — worth keeping that way
# once the field study has participants.
MODEL_TRANSCRIBE=cavi/faster-whisper-large-v3
MODEL_FAST=cavi/gemma3:12b

# Crystallisation quality matters more than speed here, so this one defaults to
# a hosted model. Switching to e.g. cavi/gpt-oss:20b or natai/kimi-k2.5 keeps
# every transcript inside AU — one env var, no code change.
MODEL_REASONING=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5

# Self-hosted models cold-start: a model that has gone idle can take 30-60s on
# the first request, then answer in well under a second. Expect the first chunk
# of a drive to lag if nobody else has used that model recently. Measured: 62s
# cold vs 227ms warm on MODEL_FAST.

# --- Talk-back (realtime conversation) --------------------------------------
# Only apps/pipecat and the /api/realtime/* routes read these. Leave
# TALKBACK_ENABLED unset and the recorder behaves exactly as before — capture
# never depends on any of this.
#
# Run `pnpm spike:talkback` to measure these against your proxy before trusting
# them. It prints a per-stage latency budget and says plainly what is too slow.
TALKBACK_ENABLED=false
# The browser half of the same switch. Separate because NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined
# into the client bundle at build time — set BOTH, to the same value, or the
# recorder will open a socket that the service refuses (or vice versa).
NEXT_PUBLIC_TALKBACK_ENABLED=false

# ASR for the live conversation, SEPARATE from the ledger's on purpose.
#
# The worker transcribes 10s chunks at batchSize 4 continuously during a drive,
# so a live turn sharing that deployment queues behind them: measured 1.7s idle
# versus 7.9-10.2s while the chunk pipeline runs. That is the difference between
# a conversation and a broken feature, and it is invisible from the UI.
#
# The ledger keeps large-v3 — quality matters there and latency does not. The
# live path wants a small model on its own queue: latency matters and quality
# does not, because live ASR is a working copy that is NEVER written to the
# utterance ledger. Falls back to MODEL_TRANSCRIBE if unset.
#
# RE-MEASURED 2026-08-25 — the numbers above are stale and this is now much
# less urgent than it reads:
#
#   ASR, 5s turn, idle                        0.95-1.08s  (was 1.7-1.8s)
#   ASR, 5s turn, behind 4 concurrent chunks  3.2s worst, 0.97s on the next turn
#
# The deployment got faster. Contention costs ~2.2s, not ~8s, and setting
# TRANSCRIBE_CONCURRENCY=1 mitigates most of what remains without any
# infrastructure change at all. Prefer that over the ask below.
#
# `cavi/faster-distil-whisper-large-v3` DOES NOT EXIST on this proxy — do not
# copy the old value here, it silently falls back. The nearest real thing is:
#
#   nugget/whisper -> deepdml/faster-whisper-large-v3-turbo-ct2 @ localhost:11430
#
# which is a genuinely separate deployment running a turbo model — exactly what
# this role wants. As of 2026-08-25 its backend returns 500 (6-14s to fail), so
# it is registered but unusable. If it is ever fixed, this becomes a one-line
# change and the ask is "fix nugget/whisper", not "deploy a new model".
#
# Note cavi/transcription and warp/transcription are ALIASES of the same
# localhost:8000 backend as MODEL_TRANSCRIBE — they contend identically and are
# useless for this role.
# MODEL_TRANSCRIBE_LIVE=nugget/whisper

# The conversation model. Deliberately NOT MODEL_FAST, for two measured reasons.
#
# cavi/gemma3:12b accepts a `tools` parameter without erroring and then never
# calls one — an agent that silently refuses to look anything up, with no error
# to explain why. And on the same retrieved context it restates transcription
# artefacts as fact ("you're going to show how to build a real-world computer"),
# where claude-sonnet-5 recognises the transcript is garbled and says so — while
# also being FASTER (2.6s vs 3.4s), because the bottleneck is prompt processing
# on a small self-hosted model, not the network.
#
# No privacy change in switching: MODEL_REASONING already sends transcript to
# Anthropic for workspace extraction, so the same speech already leaves AU. It
# does cost more — this runs on every conversational turn, where extraction
# batches eight utterances — so watch the spend on a long deployment.
#
# REVISITED 11 SEP 2026, with the eval harness (pnpm talkback:eval), because
# "responses are too slow" was the complaint and this is the largest single
# term in it: cavi/medium measured 214ms for a full turn where
# claude-sonnet-5 took 1943ms. On the same 22-case suite, talkback-6 prompt,
# one run each:
#
#   cavi/medium            checks 21/22   judge grounding 4.38
#   anthropic/claude-sonnet-5  checks 19/22   judge grounding 4.95
#
# The fast model is BETTER on the mechanical contract (sonnet spoke a speaker
# tag and broke the word cap; the fast model did not) and weaker on judged
# grounding — it justified an opinion with invented specifics and restated a
# garbled fragment rather than flagging it. The one hallucination that matters
# most — inventing something from the transcript — both models decline
# cleanly (recall-absent passes on both). The live default is now the fast
# model; switch back by restoring the sonnet line, and re-run the eval when
# the prompt changes — the two reports from 11 Sep are in the repo history
# of this decision.
#
# There is no fallback for this role: an unset value fails loudly instead.
MODEL_CONVERSE=cavi/medium

# --- MODEL_EMBED: the memory index — OPTIONAL --------------------------------
# Talk-back's recall across drives is lexical by default: it finds the word, not
# the thought. Set an embedding model and the worker builds a memory index —
# every ended drive cut into ~40s passages, and every workspace topic rendered
# as "where things stand" — and each turn's recall adds a semantic arm plus the
# state of the topics the turn touches. See TALKBACK.md, "Memory".
#
# UNSET IS OFF, and off is exactly the behaviour before this existed. There is
# no fallback role: an embedding model is not interchangeable with a chat one.
#
# The DIMENSION is not pinned anywhere; rows store the model that made them and
# search sees only the current model's rows. So changing this value silently
# empties recall until `pnpm memory:reindex` has run — do both together.
#
# A self-hosted model keeps participant speech at AU; a hosted one sends every
# passage and every query out, the same ethics line as STT_PROVIDER. The web
# app needs this too, to embed the query on each turn.
# MODEL_EMBED=cavi/nomic-embed-text

# --- MODEL_SUMMARISE: the drive's rolling summary ---------------------------
# Folds live speech into a running summary of the drive (RunningSummary in
# apps/pipecat/bot.py), which is what the agent uses for "what have we covered
# so far" instead of a raw dump of the ledger.
#
# Unset falls back to MODEL_CONVERSE, and that direction is deliberate: it must
# NOT fall back to MODEL_FAST. Every fold builds on the previous one, so a model
# that restates a transcription artefact as fact turns the summary into a
# hallucination amplifier — a fabrication introduced at turn three is still
# there at turn forty, and the ledger cannot correct it. The call is small
# (~90 words out), so a better model here costs almost nothing.
# MODEL_SUMMARISE=

# --- Voice (ElevenLabs) — REQUIRED for talk-back ----------------------------
# Text to speech happens INSIDE the Pipecat container, against ElevenLabs' own
# streaming websocket — not through LiteLLM. There is no MODEL_SPEAK role any
# more: an OpenAI-shaped envelope has nowhere to put the parameters that matter
# for a conversational voice, and the container needs a socket it can feed
# incrementally rather than a request per sentence.
#
# `apps/pipecat/bot.py` reads the key and voice with os.environ[...], so a
# missing value is a startup crash, not a silent degradation. Both are required.
#
# This sends the agent's words — which quote the participant's own thinking back
# to them — to a US vendor. An ethics-form line, and a smaller step than
# STT_PROVIDER below, which sends the audio itself.
#
# WHICH VOICE. The recorder offers the voices in
# packages/talkback/src/voice.ts and stores the choice on the capture session;
# the container speaks in whatever /api/realtime/session hands it. This
# variable is the FALLBACK for a session with no choice — every recording from
# before the picker existed, and every degraded connection — and it is still
# required at boot. Set it to one of the catalogue ids so the fallback is a
# voice the study has heard:
#
#   XHqlxleHbYnK8xmft8Vq   Voice A
#   x86DtpnPPuq2BpEiKPRy   Voice B
#   A9evEp8yGjv4c3WsIKuY   Voice C
#
# Adding or renaming a voice is an edit to voice.ts, not to this file.
#
# eleven_flash_v2_5 is the low-latency model; eleven_v3 trades ~175ms for
# noticeably better prosody, which matters more here than the latency does —
# TTS is ~400ms of an 11-second turn.
#
# MODEL CHOICE IS CONSTRAINED BY THE TRANSPORT, not by preference. Continuous
# speech needs the streaming-input WEBSOCKET — one synthesis fed incrementally,
# rather than a request per sentence, which is what makes the difference between
# speech and stitched-together fragments. Measured against this key:
#
#   eleven_v3          403 on the websocket — HTTP-only, unusable here
#   eleven_turbo_v2_5  works, better prosody than flash
#   eleven_flash_v2_5  works, lowest latency
#
# eleven_v3 sounds best over plain HTTP and cannot be used at all for a live
# conversation. Turbo is the default for that reason.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=
ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID=XHqlxleHbYnK8xmft8Vq
# ELEVENLABS_MODEL_ID=eleven_turbo_v2_5

# --- Talk-back container ----------------------------------------------------
# Where the Pipecat container reaches the web app, for /api/realtime/session,
# /api/realtime/context and /api/realtime/agent-turn.
#
# NOT localhost: inside a container that is the container. On Docker Desktop the
# host gateway is the default below; on Linux the compose file maps
# host.docker.internal via extra_hosts so the same value works.
# WEB_URL=http://host.docker.internal:3000

# The port the container listens on. Must match NEXT_PUBLIC_PIPECAT_URL, which
# is what the BROWSER dials — the two are different sides of the same hop.
# PIPECAT_PORT=7860

# --- Storage ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Audio is TRANSIENT: a chunk lives here only until its transcript is committed,
# then it is deleted. The transcript is the record; the audio is scaffolding.
# Expect this directory to sit near empty.
#
# Set KEEP_AUDIO=true to retain it instead. Worth doing before you change
# CHUNK_MS or MODEL_TRANSCRIBE — once audio is gone a corpus cannot be
# re-derived, and the transcript is all you will ever be able to analyse.
# KEEP_AUDIO=true
#
# In production this is a Coolify persistent volume (/data/storage).
#
# MUST BE ABSOLUTE. Web, worker and the seed scripts each run from their own
# working directory, so a relative path would scatter one session's audio across
# three folders — with no error, just a corpus that quietly does not line up.
STORAGE_DIR=/absolute/path/to/voice-workspace/storage

# --- Auth (Better Auth) -----------------------------------------------------
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-me
# The public origin, with NO port suffix in production and no trailing slash.
# Better Auth builds the OAuth callback and validates request origins from this,
# so it must match what the browser actually asks for and match the callback
# registered on the GitHub app. In production set it by hand rather than deriving
# it from Coolify's SERVICE_FQDN_WEB_3000, which includes the container port.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Social sign-in is OPTIONAL and per-provider. Leave a pair empty and that
# button does not appear; leave both empty and the app runs guest-only, with no
# OAuth app to register before you can record. Fill them in when you want
# recordings to survive a cleared cookie or a second device.
#
# Accounts with the same VERIFIED email are linked across providers (see
# accountLinking in apps/web/src/lib/auth.ts). Without that, one person signing
# in with Google on a phone and GitHub on a laptop becomes two users and their
# growth curve splits in half.
#
# GitHub: register TWO apps, one per origin — a single app cannot serve both
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/github
# and the deployed domain. Testing the recorder on a real phone needs a tunnel
# (`pnpm tunnel`), whose URL must also be registered.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Google: ONE client is enough — unlike GitHub it accepts several redirect URIs.
# Add both http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google and the deployed
# domain's. Scopes are openid/email/profile only, which are non-sensitive and so
# need no Google verification review.
#
# Set the consent screen to PUBLISHED, not Testing. In Testing only listed test
# users can sign in (100 max) and refresh tokens expire after 7 days — which
# breaks a longitudinal study weeks in, long after anyone is looking.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Analytics (PostHog) — OPTIONAL -----------------------------------------
# Leave the token empty and every capture call becomes a no-op, in both the
# browser and the worker. A fresh clone still runs with nothing but a database.
#
# The project token is NOT a secret: it is a write-only ingest key that ships in
# the HTML of every page. It is a build arg rather than runtime config only
# because Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_* at build time — see apps/web/Dockerfile.
#
# EU cloud. The study records people thinking aloud, so keep the region in the
# EU alongside the rest of the data.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN=
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://eu.i.posthog.com

# Send the transcript excerpts and model responses to PostHog as $ai_input /
# $ai_output_choices, so AI Observability shows what the model actually saw.
# Set false to keep every generation metadata-only (model, tokens, latency,
# cost) with no participant speech leaving this deployment.
POSTHOG_CAPTURE_AI_CONTENT=true

# --- Workspace extraction ---------------------------------------------------
# Utterances per extraction batch. FIXED on purpose: batch boundaries have to be
# a pure function of the transcript, or a rebuild slices the same speech
# differently, misses the extraction cache and pays for fresh model calls.
WORKSPACE_BATCH_SIZE=8

# --- Talk-back transport ----------------------------------------------------
# Talk-back runs on Pipecat (apps/pipecat), peer-to-peer WebRTC with no media
# server. It replaced a hand-rolled WebSocket service, and then won a head-to-
# head against a LiveKit implementation which has since been deleted — see the
# decision record in TALKBACK.md.
#
# Locally: docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.pipecat.yml up -d
# or `pnpm talkback:up`, which is the same thing.
#
# WebRTC here is peer-to-peer, so the media never traverses a server you run —
# but that also means it needs a direct UDP path. Fine on localhost; behind NAT
# in production it needs a TURN server.
NEXT_PUBLIC_PIPECAT_URL=http://localhost:7860

# --- Live transcription provider --------------------------------------------
# ASR is the whole latency problem: measured 1.7s to first text with the GPU
# free and 11.1s when the batch chunk pipeline was using the same
# faster-whisper-large-v3 deployment. A streaming provider changes the shape —
# transcription finishes as you stop talking rather than starting then — and it
# runs on someone else's hardware, so the contention spikes go away.
#
#   litellm     (default) Whisper on AU infrastructure. Nothing leaves.
#   deepgram    nova-3, streaming, ~300ms. US-hosted.
#   assemblyai  Universal-Streaming, ~300ms. US-hosted.
#
# 🔴 deepgram/assemblyai send RAW PARTICIPANT AUDIO out of the deployment. That
# is an ethics-application matter, not a config detail. Note the scope though:
# only the LIVE conversation goes to the provider. The durable ledger is still
# transcribed by Whisper at AU in apps/worker, and `utterance` never contains a
# word the live path produced — so the paper's primary artefact stays
# AU-derived either way.
STT_PROVIDER=litellm
DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=
DEEPGRAM_MODEL=nova-3
# Deepgram's own endpointing, kept near Silero's stop_secs so the feel matches.
# Raise it if it starts cutting people off mid-thought.
DEEPGRAM_UTTERANCE_END_MS=1000
ASSEMBLYAI_API_KEY=

# Fallback language for the live path when a drive states none (BCP-47: "en",
# "de", "nl-NL"). Unset = auto-detect: Deepgram "multi" (per-word
# code-switching), AssemblyAI per-turn detection, Whisper auto-detect. Auto is
# the right default for a mixed German/English corpus — a fixed Deepgram
# language does not merely mishear other languages, it returns NOTHING for
# them (verified: German speech + language=en came back an empty transcript, +
# language=multi came back perfect German).
#
# PER-DRIVE CHOICES OVERRIDE THIS: the recorder offers a language picker
# (Auto / English / Deutsch), the choice is stored on
# capture_session.stt_language, and BOTH paths honour it — the live STT here
# (via /api/realtime/session) and the ledger Whisper in apps/worker, which
# otherwise auto-detects. This var is the fallback only: drives recorded
# before the picker existed, and degraded connections with no ticket.
STT_LANGUAGE=

# Tell speakers apart on the live path. Deepgram labels every word, AssemblyAI
# every turn; once a SECOND voice is heard, bot.py tags transcripts
# "[Speaker N]" for the model, the summary and the screen. A no-op on the
# litellm default — Whisper returns text and nothing else — and never applied
# to the ledger, which is Whisper regardless. See TALKBACK.md, "Who is talking".
STT_DIARIZE=true

# The proactive engine (Offers in bot.py): unprompted turns — one short line
# when the drive starts, and one offer out of a silence long enough that a
# thought has clearly settled (25s driving / 12s walking / 7s desk, from the
# setting's proactivity level). The engine only creates the moment; the model
# still decides whether anything is worth saying, via <silence> as always,
# and a declined offer backs off. Set false to get the purely reactive
# behaviour back for a study arm.
PROACTIVE_OFFERS=true

# --- DEPLOYMENT ONLY (Coolify) ----------------------------------------------
# Not used by `pnpm dev`, and REQUIRED when deploying.

# The public domain, WITHOUT scheme — e.g. voice.example.com. Interpolated into
# the Traefik router rule for the pipecat service, which is routed on the same
# origin as the web app under /pipecat so the browser's request is same-origin.
#
# There is no sensible default and no error if it is missing: the rule renders
# as Host(``) and talk-back becomes silently unroutable, while web, worker and
# capture all keep working normally. Set it to the same host as BETTER_AUTH_URL.
REALTIME_HOST=voice.example.com

# Where the BROWSER dials the talk-back container. Must match the Traefik route
# above, and must be https — a secure page cannot open a WebRTC connection
# whose signalling is plain http.
#
# Inlined into the client bundle at build time, so changing it needs a rebuild,
# not a restart.
#   local: http://localhost:7860      production: https://<REALTIME_HOST>/pipecat
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PIPECAT_URL=https://voice.example.com/pipecat

# How the audio finds a path. Only signalling goes through Traefik; the media is
# peer-to-peer, so in production this container needs STUN to discover its
# public mapping — without it ICE never completes and every call times out after
# ~40s looking like a network fault. Comma-separated; TURN goes here too if STUN
# is not enough. See the note in apps/pipecat/bot.py.
# ICE_SERVERS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302

# The BROWSER's half of the ICE configuration. Mirrors ICE_SERVERS above, and is
# needed for the same reason: only signalling goes over HTTPS, the media is
# peer-to-peer, so a browser behind a home router must learn its own public
# mapping or it offers nothing the container can reach.
#
# Omitting this is invisible on a LAN — host candidates are directly reachable
# there — and breaks every remote deployment, with the call stalling in
# `checking` until it times out.
#
# Inlined at build time. Comma-separated; TURN goes here too.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ICE_SERVERS=stun:stun.l.google.com:19302

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Langfuse — seeing the prompt that was actually sent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The composed prompt is assembled across two services: composeSystemPrompt
# sandwiches the base identity, the setting stanza and the output contract, and
# /api/realtime/context appends recalled passages per turn. Until this existed
# the only record of what reached the model was a character count in a log line,
# which cannot separate a bad reply caused by a bad prompt from one caused by a
# bad model.
#
# Set both keys and the talk-back container exports Pipecat's OpenTelemetry
# spans — STT, LLM and TTS per turn, with the serialised messages on each LLM
# span — to Langfuse. The drive's `capture_session.id` is the trace's
# conversation id, so a trace joins to `utterance` and `agent_turn` by an id the
# ledger already has.
#
# UNSET IS OFF, and off costs nothing: no exporter, no spans, and the drive does
# not care whether Langfuse is reachable.
#
# THE ETHICS LINE, same as STT_PROVIDER: the spans carry participant speech —
# the recalled passages and the transcribed turn. Langfuse Cloud is outside AU;
# self-host it, or accept that as a form line.
#
# The same keys let `pnpm talkback:eval` post its evaluated turns — reply,
# judge, scores — into the same project, so a prompt version can be compared
# across drives and offline runs. See TALKBACK.md, "Evaluating the prompt".
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-...
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-...
# LANGFUSE_HOST=https://cloud.langfuse.com
